2. beam -- glass (n 1.5) onto the centred line (wc 1, gam 0.1, g 0.05), s polarization, field at the beam centre
  kx_b(1 + i s) crosses the real axis once: s = 0.01830, kx_c = 1.000212 = 1.5 sin 41.8212 deg (section 1: 41.8212 deg)
  it ends at kx_b(1) = 1.000312 -0.024992i, below the axis, so the contour dips under it
  check: the trajectory is continuous from s_c to 0 (largest step 1.4e-06 for ds 9.2e-07)
    and below the axis there (max Im -1.2e-06)
  check: on the real axis the fixed-kx causal root of section 1 is the axis root stepped at kx_c: max |difference|
    1.1e-13 over kx 0.5 .. 1.45
  check: wavebit R vs NumPy Fresnel with Maystre's root, max |difference| 8.9e-16
    GainCutoffWarning at 42.0 deg yes, 43.3 deg yes, 43.8 deg yes, 44.2 deg yes, 44.5 deg no, 45.0 deg no
  the window: the finger's Gaussian weight at the tip, exp((W/2)^2 (Im^2 - (kx0 - Re)^2)), grows with W for
    |kx0 - Re kx_b(1)| < |Im kx_b(1)|: kx0 0.975320 .. 1.025304, theta 40.56 .. 43.12 deg,
    where the ratio is below 0.975 and 1.025 on the two sides
  the gain length L_g = 2 sqrt(Re eps) / (k0 |Im eps|) = 40, so W 100 .. 800 is W/L_g 2.5 .. 20.0
  Columns. E_r(0) and E_inc(0) are the reflected and incident fields at x = 0, the beam centre.
  |E_r/E_inc|^2 is the squared field ratio there, not a reflectance: inside the window the reflected field is a
  lateral wave that grows downstream (below). E_r takes the Briggs-Bers contour; E_M is the same beam
  under Maystre's kernel. |dev| = |E_r - E_M| / |E_inc|, the distance to that beam. |to r| = |E_r/E_inc - r|,
  the distance to the plane wave's r at the beam's centre angle. |finger| is finger B's part of dev.
  Cauchy = |dev_A - dev_B| / |dev| for the two fingers. refine = the larger relative change of dev and of
  E_M when the nodes double. A row past 1e-8 in either prints as unreliable.
